"AWS Glue":{"security":["AWS Glue should receive least-privilege data-plane access to only the catalogs, streams, indexes or workgroups it serves.","Encrypt source, intermediate and result data and constrain cross-account roles; analytics permissions do not automatically protect the underlying lake.","Audit query, schema, connector and destination changes with CloudTrail and service-native logs, while excluding sensitive query payloads from verbose logs."],"optimization":["Choose the service's native partitioning, batching, checkpointing or shard/throughput model from the access pattern instead of scaling the whole analytics stack.","Push projection, predicate, compression and time-window filtering toward the source so AWS Glue processes fewer bytes or records.","Separate interactive, streaming and scheduled workloads so bursty consumers do not starve latency-sensitive queries or pipelines."],"cost":["Model AWS Glue from its documented scan, ingestion, processing, capacity or storage unit; include catalog, connector, retention and transfer charges.","Partitioning, compression, batching and retention reduce the dominant data-volume driver more reliably than over-provisioning capacity.","Count downstream S3, Lambda, Glue, OpenSearch, Redshift or visualization charges created by each pipeline."],"watchPoints":["AWS Glue is one stage in a data path: verify schema, partitioning, checkpoints, retries and downstream permissions at the boundaries.","A successful query or ingest can still produce an incorrect result when late data, duplicates, skew or schema evolution is ignored.","Watch service quotas, lag/backlog, throttling, failed records and the freshness SLA—not just CPU or request count."]},
