"AWS AppSync":{"security":["Use AWS AppSync's resource policies, execution roles and identity controls separately; grant each producer, consumer or target only the actions it needs.","Keep secrets and regulated payloads out of broadly routed events, messages and configuration snapshots; encrypt data and restrict KMS use.","Audit routes, rules, subscriptions, state-machine changes, configuration versions and failed deliveries with CloudTrail and native diagnostics."],"optimization":["Use AWS AppSync's native filtering, batching, retries, backoff and concurrency controls to remove unnecessary invocations and duplicate work.","Choose the interaction model deliberately: durable queueing, fan-out, event routing, configuration rollout or orchestration each has different failure semantics.","Prefer direct service integrations when they remove a Lambda hop, and make every consumer idempotent before increasing parallelism."],"cost":["Primary AWS AppSync cost follows its documented requests, messages, state transitions, evaluations, connections or configuration operations.","One event or message can multiply downstream Lambda, queue, workflow, API and data-transfer charges; estimate the full fan-out path.","Filtering, batching, long polling, controlled retries and retention limits lower volume without weakening delivery guarantees."],"watchPoints":["AWS AppSync owns routing, delivery or orchestration—not the correctness of downstream business processing.","Separate retry, timeout, DLQ, replay and idempotency behavior; otherwise transient failures become duplicate side effects.","Check payload limits, ordering scope, delivery lag, quotas and the distinction between durable state and transient routing."]},
