"Amazon Managed Blockchain":{"security":["Apply least privilege to Amazon Managed Blockchain's control-plane and data-plane permissions, isolate operators from runtime identities, and encrypt sensitive inputs, outputs and logs.","Use the service's native resource policies, roles, network controls, key/secret integration and retention settings rather than broad account permissions.","Audit configuration, access, failure and lifecycle changes with CloudTrail and the service-native evidence path."],"optimization":["Tune Amazon Managed Blockchain using its documented capacity, request, data, connection, concurrency or lifecycle model and a measured workload baseline.","Use native batching, filtering, caching, autoscaling, tiering or asynchronous operation where it matches the service's behavior.","Keep adjacent state, retries, observability and recovery paths explicit so optimizing Amazon Managed Blockchain does not move the bottleneck elsewhere."],"cost":["Start with Amazon Managed Blockchain's documented billing dimensions, then include storage, transfer, logs, encryption, compute and downstream calls created by the architecture.","Right-size the dominant measured driver and expire unused capacity, artifacts, logs, copies or connections without weakening the required SLA.","Validate the estimate with workload volume, retention, retries, fan-out and Region/account placement."],"watchPoints":["Confirm exactly which boundary Amazon Managed Blockchain owns and which responsibilities remain with the calling application or adjacent AWS service.","Track the service-native quota, backlog, health, retry and scaling signals that can constrain production.","Test failure, recovery, duplication, deletion and cross-account/Region behavior before relying on the happy path."]},
"Amazon SES":{"security":["Use Amazon SES's identity, role, endpoint and tenant controls to separate operators, applications, customers and test users.","Protect recordings, messages, builds, sessions and customer data with encryption, redaction, retention and least-privilege access.","Audit configuration, routing, deployment, test and delivery changes, and keep sensitive content out of debug logs."],"optimization":["Tune Amazon SES around its native session, contact, message, build or test concurrency model; scale the bottleneck that users actually experience.","Automate repeatable flows and isolate test/staging traffic from production while preserving realistic failure and latency checks.","Use caching, batching, reusable environments and asynchronous work when the interaction does not require a synchronous path."],"cost":["Model Amazon SES from minutes, sessions, messages, builds, devices, users, storage, delivery and add-on analytics or AI features.","Retention, idle sessions/environments, retries and multi-channel fan-out often dominate; set lifecycle and concurrency limits.","Include Lambda, storage, telephony, transfer, logs and downstream integrations in the user-journey estimate."],"watchPoints":["Amazon SES sits on a customer, operator or test boundary; verify identity, consent, fallback and data-retention behavior end to end.","Watch abandonment, delivery bounce, session startup, queue/contact backlog, test flakiness and integration retries.","A green control-plane deployment does not guarantee a usable customer interaction or test result."]},
"AWS App Runner":{"security":["Apply least privilege to AWS App Runner's instance, task, pod, job or deployment roles and keep control-plane permissions separate from runtime data access.","Keep workloads in private network paths where appropriate, encrypt images, volumes, logs and secrets, and restrict administrative entry points.","Audit image, launch-template, scaling, deployment and runtime changes; scan artifacts before promotion and monitor drift."],"optimization":["Tune AWS App Runner from its native unit—vCPU/memory, task or pod density, image pull time, job queue, instance family or concurrency—using measured utilization.","Use immutable artifacts, health checks and gradual rollout controls so scaling and deployment do not trade reliability for speed.","Separate stateless front ends, durable state and asynchronous workers; scale each according to its own bottleneck."],"cost":["Model AWS App Runner using its actual runtime, capacity, request, image, storage and data-transfer dimensions rather than a single instance price.","Right-size memory/CPU and use savings mechanisms only after workload shape and interruption/availability requirements are understood.","Include load balancers, NAT, ECR storage/transfer, logs, attached storage and downstream calls in the service path estimate."],"watchPoints":["AWS App Runner's scaling boundary is not the same as the application's scaling boundary; inspect queues, databases, connection pools and quotas.","Deployment health can look green while the workload is failing downstream or serving an unhealthy dependency.","Watch startup/image-pull latency, placement capacity, throttling, failed health checks, retries and graceful draining."]},
