"Amazon Aurora":{"security":["Keep Amazon Aurora in private network paths with narrowly scoped security groups, roles and database users; never use a shared administrator credential in applications.","Encrypt storage, snapshots, replicas, streams and backups with controlled KMS keys, and restrict restore/share operations.","Audit configuration, authentication, schema/index changes, failover and administrative access; protect sensitive query and parameter logs."],"optimization":["Design Amazon Aurora from access patterns, connection behavior, indexes/keys, replication and workload class before selecting capacity or instance size.","Use pooling, caching, read scaling, partition/shard distribution and maintenance windows deliberately; measure latency and saturation at the engine layer.","Separate transactional, analytical, backup and stream consumers so one pattern cannot exhaust the primary workload's resources."],"cost":["Model Amazon Aurora across instance or request capacity, storage, I/O, backups, replicas, streams, global replication and data transfer.","Unused replicas, oversized storage, excessive indexes and long backup retention are common cost drivers; verify them against recovery and latency goals.","Strong consistency, cross-Region writes and high availability can multiply capacity or transfer; price the chosen semantics explicitly."],"watchPoints":["Amazon Aurora has service-specific consistency, failover, scaling and maintenance behavior; do not infer it from another database engine.","Hot keys, connection exhaustion, replica lag, storage pressure and slow queries can appear before CPU is high.","Backups and replicas improve recovery/availability but do not remove the need to test restore time and application failover."]},
