"AWS CDK":{"security":["Use scoped build, deploy and artifact roles for AWS CDK; separate source, build, release and production permissions so a pipeline compromise cannot reach every account.","Sign or verify artifacts, protect connection tokens and secrets, and keep credentials out of templates, logs and package metadata.","Audit pipeline, stack, environment, artifact and CLI/API changes with CloudTrail and retain provenance for released versions."],"optimization":["Use AWS CDK's native caching, parallel stages, change sets, deployment strategies and reusable constructs to reduce idle build and rollout time.","Keep environments reproducible and eliminate unnecessary rebuilds; test policy, dependency and infrastructure changes before promotion.","Make rollback, drift detection and failure isolation explicit instead of relying on a manual console repair."],"cost":["Model AWS CDK from build minutes, pipeline actions, artifact storage/transfer, API calls and the compute or environments it provisions.","Cache dependencies and expire old artifacts/logs, but preserve the retention needed for audit and rollback.","Include downstream test environments, NAT, runners, CloudFormation resources and observability charges."],"watchPoints":["AWS CDK is a delivery/control-plane service; success means an artifact or change was accepted, not that the application is healthy.","Watch drift, dependency resolution, IAM changes, rollback status, artifact immutability and cross-account trust.","Quotas, concurrency, regional support and deployment blast radius should be checked before scaling release frequency."]},
