"Amazon AppStream 2.0":{"security":["Use Amazon AppStream 2.0's identity, role, endpoint and tenant controls to separate operators, applications, customers and test users.","Protect recordings, messages, builds, sessions and customer data with encryption, redaction, retention and least-privilege access.","Audit configuration, routing, deployment, test and delivery changes, and keep sensitive content out of debug logs."],"optimization":["Tune Amazon AppStream 2.0 around its native session, contact, message, build or test concurrency model; scale the bottleneck that users actually experience.","Automate repeatable flows and isolate test/staging traffic from production while preserving realistic failure and latency checks.","Use caching, batching, reusable environments and asynchronous work when the interaction does not require a synchronous path."],"cost":["Model Amazon AppStream 2.0 from minutes, sessions, messages, builds, devices, users, storage, delivery and add-on analytics or AI features.","Retention, idle sessions/environments, retries and multi-channel fan-out often dominate; set lifecycle and concurrency limits.","Include Lambda, storage, telephony, transfer, logs and downstream integrations in the user-journey estimate."],"watchPoints":["Amazon AppStream 2.0 sits on a customer, operator or test boundary; verify identity, consent, fallback and data-retention behavior end to end.","Watch abandonment, delivery bounce, session startup, queue/contact backlog, test flakiness and integration retries.","A green control-plane deployment does not guarantee a usable customer interaction or test result."]},
"Amazon API Gateway":{"security":["Control Amazon API Gateway at the network, listener, route, endpoint and identity boundaries; use private paths and least-privilege security groups/policies where possible.","Terminate and re-encrypt TLS deliberately, protect origin/backend credentials and use WAF or equivalent controls for internet-facing paths.","Audit route, listener, endpoint, DNS, certificate and policy changes; log enough metadata to trace a request without exposing payloads."],"optimization":["Choose Amazon API Gateway's routing, caching, health-check, load-balancing, endpoint and connection behavior from latency and failure-domain requirements.","Reduce cross-AZ/Region and internet hops, reuse connections, tune cache keys and health checks, and isolate noisy tenants or routes.","Use weighted/failover/latency routing or staged rollout only when health signals and rollback behavior are defined."],"cost":["Model Amazon API Gateway from requests, connections, bytes, endpoints, hours, health checks, acceleration, transfer and attached inspection/logging services.","Cross-AZ and cross-Region transfer can outweigh the front-door charge; measure path placement before optimizing unit rates.","Cache, aggregate, right-size and remove idle endpoints/listeners while retaining the availability target."],"watchPoints":["Amazon API Gateway controls reachability and delivery, but backend authorization and application correctness remain separate.","Watch DNS TTL, route convergence, target health, connection exhaustion, MTU/TLS mismatches, throttling and asymmetric paths.","Document the failure domain and test failover from the client’s perspective, not only from control-plane status."]},
"AWS IoT Core":{"security":["Use per-device identities, least-privilege policies and certificate lifecycle controls for AWS IoT Core; never share a fleet-wide credential.","Encrypt device traffic and protect firmware, shadows, telemetry and commands according to device and data sensitivity.","Audit policy, thing, rule, job and certificate changes, and separate device onboarding from operator administration."],"optimization":["Batch telemetry, filter at the edge/rule boundary and use fleet groups/jobs to avoid per-device control-plane chatter.","Choose retained messages, shadows, rules and storage destinations from the device interaction pattern rather than enabling every feature globally.","Design for intermittent connectivity and bounded device resources; backoff and offline queues protect both devices and the service."],"cost":["Model AWS IoT Core from messages, connections, rules/jobs, device registry operations, storage and downstream delivery.","Payload size, message frequency, fan-out and retention usually dominate; compact schemas and edge filtering reduce the whole pipeline.","Include Timestream, Kinesis, Lambda, S3, logs and network charges created by device data."],"watchPoints":["AWS IoT Core crosses an unreliable device boundary, so authentication, reconnect, ordering and duplicate handling are first-class concerns.","A rule or job can fan out to many devices or destinations; validate blast radius before production rollout.","Track rejected messages, connection churn, rule failures, job rollout status, quotas and firmware compatibility."]},
