"Amazon Kinesis Video Streams":{"security":["Amazon Kinesis Video Streams should receive least-privilege data-plane access to only the catalogs, streams, indexes or workgroups it serves.","Encrypt source, intermediate and result data and constrain cross-account roles; analytics permissions do not automatically protect the underlying lake.","Audit query, schema, connector and destination changes with CloudTrail and service-native logs, while excluding sensitive query payloads from verbose logs."],"optimization":["Choose the service's native partitioning, batching, checkpointing or shard/throughput model from the access pattern instead of scaling the whole analytics stack.","Push projection, predicate, compression and time-window filtering toward the source so Amazon Kinesis Video Streams processes fewer bytes or records.","Separate interactive, streaming and scheduled workloads so bursty consumers do not starve latency-sensitive queries or pipelines."],"cost":["Model Amazon Kinesis Video Streams from its documented scan, ingestion, processing, capacity or storage unit; include catalog, connector, retention and transfer charges.","Partitioning, compression, batching and retention reduce the dominant data-volume driver more reliably than over-provisioning capacity.","Count downstream S3, Lambda, Glue, OpenSearch, Redshift or visualization charges created by each pipeline."],"watchPoints":["Amazon Kinesis Video Streams is one stage in a data path: verify schema, partitioning, checkpoints, retries and downstream permissions at the boundaries.","A successful query or ingest can still produce an incorrect result when late data, duplicates, skew or schema evolution is ignored.","Watch service quotas, lag/backlog, throttling, failed records and the freshness SLA—not just CPU or request count."]},
"AWS CloudFormation":{"security":["Use scoped build, deploy and artifact roles for AWS CloudFormation; separate source, build, release and production permissions so a pipeline compromise cannot reach every account.","Sign or verify artifacts, protect connection tokens and secrets, and keep credentials out of templates, logs and package metadata.","Audit pipeline, stack, environment, artifact and CLI/API changes with CloudTrail and retain provenance for released versions."],"optimization":["Use AWS CloudFormation's native caching, parallel stages, change sets, deployment strategies and reusable constructs to reduce idle build and rollout time.","Keep environments reproducible and eliminate unnecessary rebuilds; test policy, dependency and infrastructure changes before promotion.","Make rollback, drift detection and failure isolation explicit instead of relying on a manual console repair."],"cost":["Model AWS CloudFormation from build minutes, pipeline actions, artifact storage/transfer, API calls and the compute or environments it provisions.","Cache dependencies and expire old artifacts/logs, but preserve the retention needed for audit and rollback.","Include downstream test environments, NAT, runners, CloudFormation resources and observability charges."],"watchPoints":["AWS CloudFormation is a delivery/control-plane service; success means an artifact or change was accepted, not that the application is healthy.","Watch drift, dependency resolution, IAM changes, rollback status, artifact immutability and cross-account trust.","Quotas, concurrency, regional support and deployment blast radius should be checked before scaling release frequency."]},
"AWS CloudTrail":{"security":["Restrict AWS CloudTrail administration with delegated roles and least privilege, and separate observation from mutation permissions.","Protect logs, findings, configuration snapshots, automation documents and assessment data with encryption, retention and access controls.","Audit changes to controls, alarms, automation, organizational policies, accounts and delegated administration with CloudTrail."],"optimization":["Use AWS CloudTrail's native aggregation, baselines, automation, dashboards, remediation and anomaly signals to focus operators on actionable variance.","Set sampling, retention, evaluation frequency and alarm scope from the decision latency required; more telemetry is not automatically better.","Automate repeatable remediation with approvals and rollback, and keep service ownership clear across accounts and Regions."],"cost":["Model AWS CloudTrail from its documented events, metrics, logs, evaluations, checks, automation executions, storage and account/organization scope.","Filter noisy telemetry, expire old logs/snapshots and centralize only the data needed for audit, operations or optimization.","Include the downstream compute, storage, notifications and remediation actions triggered by findings or alarms."],"watchPoints":["AWS CloudTrail observes or governs other services, so stale data, missing permissions or an unscoped Region/account can create false confidence.","Watch delivery gaps, metric/log cardinality, control drift, failed automation, quota limits and alert fatigue.","Validate ownership and remediation safety before enabling organization-wide controls."]},
