"AWS Application Migration Service":{"security":["Apply least privilege to AWS Application Migration Service's control-plane and data-plane permissions, isolate operators from runtime identities, and encrypt sensitive inputs, outputs and logs.","Use the service's native resource policies, roles, network controls, key/secret integration and retention settings rather than broad account permissions.","Audit configuration, access, failure and lifecycle changes with CloudTrail and the service-native evidence path."],"optimization":["Tune AWS Application Migration Service using its documented capacity, request, data, connection, concurrency or lifecycle model and a measured workload baseline.","Use native batching, filtering, caching, autoscaling, tiering or asynchronous operation where it matches the service's behavior.","Keep adjacent state, retries, observability and recovery paths explicit so optimizing AWS Application Migration Service does not move the bottleneck elsewhere."],"cost":["Start with AWS Application Migration Service's documented billing dimensions, then include storage, transfer, logs, encryption, compute and downstream calls created by the architecture.","Right-size the dominant measured driver and expire unused capacity, artifacts, logs, copies or connections without weakening the required SLA.","Validate the estimate with workload volume, retention, retries, fan-out and Region/account placement."],"watchPoints":["Confirm exactly which boundary AWS Application Migration Service owns and which responsibilities remain with the calling application or adjacent AWS service.","Track the service-native quota, backlog, health, retry and scaling signals that can constrain production.","Test failure, recovery, duplication, deletion and cross-account/Region behavior before relying on the happy path."]},
