"Amazon CloudFront":{"security":["Control Amazon CloudFront at the network, listener, route, endpoint and identity boundaries; use private paths and least-privilege security groups/policies where possible.","Terminate and re-encrypt TLS deliberately, protect origin/backend credentials and use WAF or equivalent controls for internet-facing paths.","Audit route, listener, endpoint, DNS, certificate and policy changes; log enough metadata to trace a request without exposing payloads."],"optimization":["Choose Amazon CloudFront's routing, caching, health-check, load-balancing, endpoint and connection behavior from latency and failure-domain requirements.","Reduce cross-AZ/Region and internet hops, reuse connections, tune cache keys and health checks, and isolate noisy tenants or routes.","Use weighted/failover/latency routing or staged rollout only when health signals and rollback behavior are defined."],"cost":["Model Amazon CloudFront from requests, connections, bytes, endpoints, hours, health checks, acceleration, transfer and attached inspection/logging services.","Cross-AZ and cross-Region transfer can outweigh the front-door charge; measure path placement before optimizing unit rates.","Cache, aggregate, right-size and remove idle endpoints/listeners while retaining the availability target."],"watchPoints":["Amazon CloudFront controls reachability and delivery, but backend authorization and application correctness remain separate.","Watch DNS TTL, route convergence, target health, connection exhaustion, MTU/TLS mismatches, throttling and asymmetric paths.","Document the failure domain and test failover from the client’s perspective, not only from control-plane status."]},
