"AWS Certificate Manager":{"security":["AWS Certificate Manager is a security control boundary: apply least privilege, explicit resource scope, separation of duties and controlled key/secret access.","Encrypt sensitive data in transit and at rest, protect findings and audit records, and avoid placing credentials or regulated payloads in logs.","Enable CloudTrail and the service-native evidence path; route findings to accountable owners with documented response procedures."],"optimization":["Tune AWS Certificate Manager from the threat, trust, key, policy, rule, finding or inspection path it controls; avoid broad allow/deny changes made only to silence alerts.","Centralize shared controls where the service supports it, but test account/Region exceptions and service-to-service dependencies before rollout.","Use automation for repeatable low-risk remediation and preserve human approval for destructive or access-expanding actions."],"cost":["Model AWS Certificate Manager from requests, keys, secrets, protected resources, findings, inspections, logs, storage and account/Region coverage.","Reduce noisy rules, duplicate scans and unnecessary retention only after confirming required detection and audit coverage.","Include KMS, CloudTrail, Security Hub, SIEM, NAT, inspection endpoints and remediation compute in the control-path estimate."],"watchPoints":["AWS Certificate Manager detects, authorizes, encrypts or filters; it does not replace the application’s own identity, data-classification and recovery design.","Watch coverage gaps, policy shadowing, key/secret rotation, finding volume, false positives and service quotas.","Test failure modes: a blocked request, unavailable key, expired certificate or missing permission must fail safely and observably."]},
