"Amazon CloudWatch Synthetics":{"security":["Restrict Amazon CloudWatch Synthetics administration with delegated roles and least privilege, and separate observation from mutation permissions.","Protect logs, findings, configuration snapshots, automation documents and assessment data with encryption, retention and access controls.","Audit changes to controls, alarms, automation, organizational policies, accounts and delegated administration with CloudTrail."],"optimization":["Use Amazon CloudWatch Synthetics's native aggregation, baselines, automation, dashboards, remediation and anomaly signals to focus operators on actionable variance.","Set sampling, retention, evaluation frequency and alarm scope from the decision latency required; more telemetry is not automatically better.","Automate repeatable remediation with approvals and rollback, and keep service ownership clear across accounts and Regions."],"cost":["Model Amazon CloudWatch Synthetics from its documented events, metrics, logs, evaluations, checks, automation executions, storage and account/organization scope.","Filter noisy telemetry, expire old logs/snapshots and centralize only the data needed for audit, operations or optimization.","Include the downstream compute, storage, notifications and remediation actions triggered by findings or alarms."],"watchPoints":["Amazon CloudWatch Synthetics observes or governs other services, so stale data, missing permissions or an unscoped Region/account can create false confidence.","Watch delivery gaps, metric/log cardinality, control drift, failed automation, quota limits and alert fatigue.","Validate ownership and remediation safety before enabling organization-wide controls."]},
"Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)":{"security":["Control Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) at the network, listener, route, endpoint and identity boundaries; use private paths and least-privilege security groups/policies where possible.","Terminate and re-encrypt TLS deliberately, protect origin/backend credentials and use WAF or equivalent controls for internet-facing paths.","Audit route, listener, endpoint, DNS, certificate and policy changes; log enough metadata to trace a request without exposing payloads."],"optimization":["Choose Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)'s routing, caching, health-check, load-balancing, endpoint and connection behavior from latency and failure-domain requirements.","Reduce cross-AZ/Region and internet hops, reuse connections, tune cache keys and health checks, and isolate noisy tenants or routes.","Use weighted/failover/latency routing or staged rollout only when health signals and rollback behavior are defined."],"cost":["Model Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) from requests, connections, bytes, endpoints, hours, health checks, acceleration, transfer and attached inspection/logging services.","Cross-AZ and cross-Region transfer can outweigh the front-door charge; measure path placement before optimizing unit rates.","Cache, aggregate, right-size and remove idle endpoints/listeners while retaining the availability target."],"watchPoints":["Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) controls reachability and delivery, but backend authorization and application correctness remain separate.","Watch DNS TTL, route convergence, target health, connection exhaustion, MTU/TLS mismatches, throttling and asymmetric paths.","Document the failure domain and test failover from the client’s perspective, not only from control-plane status."]},
"AWS Encryption SDK":{"security":["AWS Encryption SDK is a security control boundary: apply least privilege, explicit resource scope, separation of duties and controlled key/secret access.","Encrypt sensitive data in transit and at rest, protect findings and audit records, and avoid placing credentials or regulated payloads in logs.","Enable CloudTrail and the service-native evidence path; route findings to accountable owners with documented response procedures."],"optimization":["Tune AWS Encryption SDK from the threat, trust, key, policy, rule, finding or inspection path it controls; avoid broad allow/deny changes made only to silence alerts.","Centralize shared controls where the service supports it, but test account/Region exceptions and service-to-service dependencies before rollout.","Use automation for repeatable low-risk remediation and preserve human approval for destructive or access-expanding actions."],"cost":["Model AWS Encryption SDK from requests, keys, secrets, protected resources, findings, inspections, logs, storage and account/Region coverage.","Reduce noisy rules, duplicate scans and unnecessary retention only after confirming required detection and audit coverage.","Include KMS, CloudTrail, Security Hub, SIEM, NAT, inspection endpoints and remediation compute in the control-path estimate."],"watchPoints":["AWS Encryption SDK detects, authorizes, encrypts or filters; it does not replace the application’s own identity, data-classification and recovery design.","Watch coverage gaps, policy shadowing, key/secret rotation, finding volume, false positives and service quotas.","Test failure modes: a blocked request, unavailable key, expired certificate or missing permission must fail safely and observably."]},
