"Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering":{"security":["Protect Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering with least-privilege data-plane roles, private endpoints or controlled transfer paths, encryption and explicit retention/deletion policies.","Restrict snapshot, replica, export, restore and sharing operations because storage copies often contain the full dataset.","Audit object/file/block, transfer, backup and recovery changes; protect the audit destination from the same failure or account boundary."],"optimization":["Choose Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering's object, block, file, cache, archive, transfer or recovery mode from access frequency, latency, throughput and recovery objectives.","Use multipart/parallel transfer, lifecycle/tiering, deduplication, compression and incremental protection where the service supports them.","Separate hot production data, staging copies, archives and recovery replicas so each uses an appropriate performance and retention tier."],"cost":["Model Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering across capacity, requests/I/O, retrieval, transfer, replication, backup, recovery, gateway/cache and lifecycle operations.","Old versions, snapshots, replicas and failed transfers accumulate silently; automate expiry and review recovery copies against the RPO/RTO.","Include KMS, CloudWatch, NAT, S3, EFS, EBS or destination compute charges created by the storage path."],"watchPoints":["Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering uses different durability, availability, consistency and recovery semantics than adjacent storage services; verify the exact contract.","Watch replication lag, retrieval delay, cache fill, capacity pressure, transfer retries, failed backups and restore time.","A copied or replicated dataset is not automatically an independent recovery plan; test isolation and deletion scenarios."]},
